## Proposed repair plan
# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:home.spec.ts::home.spec.ts_>_home_page_>_shows_the_store_name_as_its_heading

## Summary of the defect

The web service renders one static HTML document from a single template literal in `services/web/server.js`. Its top-level heading reads `Welcome` — a generic greeting — where the specification requires the store's own name. The document's `<title>` in the same template already reads `Corner Shop`, so the store name is established by the production source itself; only the `<h1>` is out of step. This is a production defect, not a test defect: the assertion is the specification.

## Evidence chain

- `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares one service, `web`, at `services/web`, language javascript, with the Playwright suite configured at `tests/playwright.config.ts`.
- `tests/playwright.config.ts` sets `webServer.command: 'node ../services/web/server.js'` and `use.baseURL: http://127.0.0.1:${port}`, where `port` is derived from `process.cwd()`. Playwright therefore starts this exact service and every `page.goto('/')` in the suite is answered by it.
- `services/web/server.js` responds to *every* request path with one constant HTML string (the handler ignores `req.url`). Line 10 of that string is `<h1>Welcome</h1>`.
- The failure is `Expected: "Corner Shop" / Received: "Welcome"` at `tests/integration/home.spec.ts:9`, which matches that line exactly.

## The change to make

**File: `services/web/server.js` (the only file that needs to change)**

In the `page` template literal, change the text content of the single `<h1>` element from `Welcome` to `Corner Shop`, so the served document's top-level heading is the store's name.

Contract the heading must satisfy:

- The document served at `/` contains exactly one `h1` element (the suite uses the bare locator `page.locator('h1')`; a second `h1` would make that locator strict-mode-ambiguous and break the test in a new way, so do not add one).
- `textContent` of that `h1` must be the exact string `Corner Shop` — no surrounding whitespace, no newline, no nested markup, no `&nbsp;` or HTML entity that decodes to anything else. The assertion is `toBe`, a strict equality on the raw `textContent`, so `\n      Corner Shop\n    ` would still fail. Keep the heading's text on one line inside the tag, exactly as `Welcome` sits there now.
- Prefer spelling the literal text directly in the markup, mirroring the existing style. If you instead introduce a constant (for example a `storeName` variable interpolated into both the `<title>` and the `<h1>`), it must produce byte-identical output for both elements — `Corner Shop` — and must not be read from an environment variable or any other runtime input, because the Playwright `webServer.env` block passes only `PORT` and nothing would supply a name at test time.

**What must not change**

- Keep `<a href="/hours">Opening hours</a>` exactly as it is, including the `href` value and the fact that it is the only `a` element in the document. The suite's second test (`home.spec.ts > home page > links to the opening hours`) asserts `page.locator('a').getAttribute('href')` is `/hours`, and that test passes today; it must still pass after the repair.
- Keep the response status `200`, the `Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8` header, and the `.listen(Number(process.env.PORT), '127.0.0.1')` call unchanged. Playwright's `webServer.url` health check polls `http://127.0.0.1:${port}/` and the suite will not start if the service stops binding that host and port or stops answering `/` with a success status.
- Keep the server answering every path with a success response. The handler currently ignores `req.url`; do not add routing or a 404 branch as part of this repair — Playwright's readiness probe and both tests only hit `/`, and narrowing the handler adds risk without serving any test in this set.
- Do not touch anything under `tests/` — not `home.spec.ts`, not `playwright.config.ts`, not `package.json`. Do not touch `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` or `true-bdd/`.

**Optional, non-behavioural tidy-up (same file, low priority)**

The comment block at the top of `services/web/server.js` currently narrates the defect ("Its heading says 'Welcome' where the store's name belongs: that is the planted defect"). Once the heading is corrected that sentence is false. You may reword those lines to describe what the service actually does — one page, served on the port `PORT` names, heading carrying the store's name. This is a comment-only edit inside the declared root with no effect on the served bytes; skip it if you prefer the minimal diff, but do not leave a comment that contradicts the code *and* also change behaviour elsewhere to match it.

